WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.968 --> 00:00:02.133
-It is so nice to see you.

00:00:02.133 --> 00:00:03.501
Welcome to the show.
Thank you.

00:00:03.501 --> 00:00:04.634
I want to talk
about "Creed III."

00:00:04.634 --> 00:00:07.234
-Okay.
-You're like the queen

00:00:07.234 --> 00:00:10.300
of movie franchises, by the way.
-No, I don't know
if that's true.

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:13.434
-Well, you're in two mass--
I mean, you're also in "Thor."

00:00:13.434 --> 00:00:14.534
-That's --
Yeah, that is true.

00:00:14.534 --> 00:00:15.300
[ Cheering and applause ]
-So, I mean --

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:16.534
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:16.534 --> 00:00:18.767
Is that exciting?
I mean,

00:00:18.767 --> 00:00:19.934
do you think about that?
-I am the king

00:00:19.934 --> 00:00:21.200
of movie franchises.
-Yes, you are the king

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:23.601
of movie franchises, sure.
-Valkyrie's the king.

00:00:23.601 --> 00:00:26.200
No, it's a trip.
I never imagined, you know.

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:28.868
But being in a movie
where there's lots of

00:00:28.868 --> 00:00:30.968
like CGI and greenscreen,

00:00:30.968 --> 00:00:33.067
that was a bucket list thing
for me.

00:00:33.067 --> 00:00:35.067
-Yeah. Is it fun?
-It's so fun.

00:00:35.067 --> 00:00:37.667
I think just, you know,
when you watch movies

00:00:37.667 --> 00:00:39.067
and you realize that,

00:00:39.067 --> 00:00:40.868
when someone's doing this,
an action scene

00:00:40.868 --> 00:00:42.100
and they're supposed to be
on a spaceship,

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:43.534
that, really,
they're in a green room

00:00:43.534 --> 00:00:45.734
and there's probably a guy
eating a sandwich.

00:00:45.734 --> 00:00:47.300
[ Laughter ]
And, you know, it's like

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:48.934
it seemed like a really
challenging thing,

00:00:48.934 --> 00:00:50.067
as an actor, so.

00:00:50.067 --> 00:00:51.501
No, they're
always eating sandwiches.

00:00:51.501 --> 00:00:53.234
[ Laughter ]
There's always guys
eating sandwiches.

00:00:53.234 --> 00:00:54.501
-Oh, is that right?
-Uh-huh.

00:00:54.501 --> 00:00:55.901
-Yeah.
-But it's a joy.

00:00:55.901 --> 00:00:57.901
And also to do films
that kids love.

00:00:57.901 --> 00:00:59.968
I feel like there are
so many parents

00:00:59.968 --> 00:01:01.801
across, you know, America,
certainly,

00:01:01.801 --> 00:01:04.000
that have selfies of me
for their children

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:05.234
and that's really sweet.
-Yeah.

00:01:05.234 --> 00:01:07.334
Yeah, you were saying you went
to the premiere

00:01:07.334 --> 00:01:08.767
of the last "Thor"
and you said you were just

00:01:08.767 --> 00:01:10.501
hanging out with everyone's kids
because they

00:01:10.501 --> 00:01:11.934
were just so excited.
-Oh, yeah. Everyone was talking

00:01:11.934 --> 00:01:14.067
to other like cool actors
that had come

00:01:14.067 --> 00:01:18.834
and I was just with like the kid
of the line producer all night.

00:01:18.834 --> 00:01:20.968
[ Laughter ]
And he's like,
"So, when you were doing this,

00:01:20.968 --> 00:01:22.234
what was really happening?"

00:01:22.234 --> 00:01:24.400
I'm like, "No, that's what
really was happening."

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:26.400
[ Laughter ]

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:28.133
Tessa Thompson,
I'm happy you're here.

00:01:28.133 --> 00:01:30.367
I read a lot about you, getting
ready for this interview,

00:01:30.367 --> 00:01:31.901
and I read something,
I said to myself,

00:01:31.901 --> 00:01:33.934
"This can't be true,"
but can you tell me

00:01:33.934 --> 00:01:35.734
if this is true or not?
-Oh, no.

00:01:35.734 --> 00:01:38.601
[ Laughter ]
-I read that you've never
eaten eggs.

00:01:38.601 --> 00:01:39.601
-That is a true thing.

00:01:39.601 --> 00:01:40.868
I've never had an egg.

00:01:40.868 --> 00:01:42.701
Hard-boiled, scrambled,

00:01:42.701 --> 00:01:44.334
omeletted.

00:01:44.334 --> 00:01:45.267
[ Laughter ]

00:01:45.267 --> 00:01:47.200
Deviled.
-Is omeletted a --

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:49.934
-Is it?
-You invented -- Wait.

00:01:49.934 --> 00:01:53.100
How have you gone through life
never having an egg?

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:54.467
-Okay, well,
I think it started

00:01:54.467 --> 00:01:56.868
because I had some
misinformation about eggs.

00:01:56.868 --> 00:01:59.567
My parents never told me
that like

00:01:59.567 --> 00:02:02.300
the eggs that you eat
would never become a chicken

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:05.567
and so, like morally,
I was like, I, you know,

00:02:05.567 --> 00:02:08.167
they should be chickens someday,
so I didn't eat eggs then.

00:02:08.167 --> 00:02:10.400
And then also, just like
once I understood that,

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:13.000
I just didn't trust them,
you know.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:14.067
[ Laughter ]

00:02:14.067 --> 00:02:16.267
Because they shape-shift.

00:02:16.267 --> 00:02:19.133
[ Laughter ]
Like one second, they're this.

00:02:19.133 --> 00:02:20.200
One second, they're that.

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:21.667
-They're a frittata.
-Yes!

00:02:21.734 --> 00:02:23.767
-Then they're an omelet.
-Yes.

00:02:23.767 --> 00:02:26.767
[ Laughter ]
It's suspect, as a kid,
if you think about it.

00:02:26.767 --> 00:02:28.200
You know what I mean?
-You go, "What is an egg?"

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:30.000
-What is an egg? And then I --
-"Is it a solid?

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:31.367
Is it a liquid?"
-I was so serious about it,

00:02:31.367 --> 00:02:33.400
I didn't even eat eggplant
for the longest time

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:34.901
[ Laughter ]
because I thought

00:02:34.901 --> 00:02:36.601
-No relation!
-it was connected.

00:02:36.601 --> 00:02:39.567
I was so committed.
But then I got to a certain age

00:02:39.567 --> 00:02:42.200
where it was kind of my shtick,
you know what I mean?

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:44.667
Like here I'm talking about it
on late night television.

00:02:44.667 --> 00:02:46.934
It's like a remarkable thing,
you know?

00:02:46.934 --> 00:02:48.534
[ Laughter ]
-So, you're kind of like --

00:02:48.534 --> 00:02:51.767
-You got to hold on to the ways
in which you can be exceptional

00:02:51.767 --> 00:02:53.734
[ Laughter ]
and for me, it's not --

00:02:53.734 --> 00:02:55.567
-This is not it for you.

00:02:55.634 --> 00:02:56.801
-For me, not --
-This is not it for you.

00:02:56.801 --> 00:02:59.367
-It's a big party trick.
-I know, but --

00:02:59.367 --> 00:03:01.100
-Seriously, I have been places
-You're in two giant

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:02.501
movie fra--
-and people are like,
"Yo, get over here.

00:03:02.501 --> 00:03:03.734
She's never had an egg!"

00:03:03.734 --> 00:03:05.601
[ Laughter ]
-That's not!

00:03:05.601 --> 00:03:07.734
What parties have you been to
where someone's like,

00:03:07.734 --> 00:03:09.434
"Get over here.
She's never had an egg"?

00:03:09.434 --> 00:03:11.167
-I have --
-That's not true.

00:03:11.167 --> 00:03:13.033
-They're really fun parties,
let me tell you.

00:03:13.033 --> 00:03:14.100
[ Laughter ]

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:16.534
-I want to be invited
to those parties.

00:03:16.534 --> 00:03:18.067
Tessa, I don't want
to put you on the spot

00:03:18.067 --> 00:03:19.868
or anything like that,
but would you ever --

00:03:19.868 --> 00:03:22.133
would you ever try an egg,
if I --

00:03:22.133 --> 00:03:23.534
-Okay. See this?
Okay.

00:03:23.534 --> 00:03:26.267
Michael B. Jordan tried to get
me to eat an egg last week

00:03:26.267 --> 00:03:28.234
[ Laughter ]
because we have a thing.

00:03:28.234 --> 00:03:31.367
We have a thing where --
First of all, Jonathan Majors

00:03:31.367 --> 00:03:33.968
ate eight hardboiled eggs
for breakfast

00:03:33.968 --> 00:03:35.167
when we were making that movie.

00:03:35.167 --> 00:03:36.601
-Yeah, because he's jacked.
-Yeah.

00:03:36.601 --> 00:03:37.834
And also, they could afford it

00:03:37.834 --> 00:03:39.234
because I wasn't eating
any eggs.

00:03:39.234 --> 00:03:40.300
[ Laughter ]

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:43.100
And then --
-You passed on them.

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:45.801
-Michael B got me to eat
my first bite of steak.

00:03:45.801 --> 00:03:47.467
I had never had a steak.
And then when we made

00:03:47.467 --> 00:03:48.467
[ Audience gasping ]
the first one --

00:03:48.467 --> 00:03:49.667
I know, I'm losing.

00:03:49.667 --> 00:03:51.701
[ Laughter ]
What do you -- What do you...

00:03:51.701 --> 00:03:53.200
-Eat?
-Is that another party trick?

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:54.601
-Yeah.
-"You got to get over here.

00:03:54.601 --> 00:03:55.534
She's never had steak."

00:03:55.534 --> 00:03:56.767
[ Laughter ]
It's like, wow,

00:03:56.767 --> 00:03:58.734
this party's getting old
real quick.
-Exactly.

00:03:58.734 --> 00:04:00.234
[ Laughter ]
-But now I don't have
those anymore,

00:04:00.234 --> 00:04:02.467
so, last week,
I declined the egg.

00:04:02.467 --> 00:04:05.100
-[ Gasps ]
-But then I thought to myself,
like, I am the kind of person,

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:07.534
like I believe in like
I'll try anything once.

00:04:07.534 --> 00:04:10.567
So, it is sort of weird
that I've never tried an egg.

00:04:10.567 --> 00:04:12.100
-And you would
do it tonight for us?

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:14.200
[ Ohhs ]
[ Drumroll ]

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:18.534
[ Cheering and applause ]

00:04:18.534 --> 00:04:19.968
-Oh, no!

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:21.834
-Tess.

00:04:24.534 --> 00:04:25.901
I don't --

00:04:25.901 --> 00:04:29.667
Tessa, I don't want you
to smell the egg or anything.

00:04:29.667 --> 00:04:32.734
-I know.
-I have ketchup as well.

00:04:32.734 --> 00:04:34.234
Yeah, but the eggs
are a different thing.

00:04:34.234 --> 00:04:36.267
I don't want you to like --
I don't want to steer you wrong.

00:04:36.267 --> 00:04:38.334
Do you have a --
-How do you eat your eggs?

00:04:40.167 --> 00:04:41.801
[ Laughter ]

00:04:41.801 --> 00:04:43.200
No one's ever came
on "The Tonight Show"

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:44.534
and asked me a question.

00:04:44.534 --> 00:04:46.701
[ Laughter ]
It feels special to me.

00:04:46.701 --> 00:04:51.167
[ Laughter ]
That was like the only party
trick I had going for me, but --

00:04:51.167 --> 00:04:53.868
[ Laughter and applause ]
But I'm ready to try
new things tonight.

00:04:53.868 --> 00:04:57.133
I like fried eggs. I like --
Sunny side up is my jam.

00:04:57.133 --> 00:04:58.434
-Oh, okay, okay.
-Yeah.

00:04:58.434 --> 00:05:00.334
Scrambled, too.
-Fried eggs look really nice.

00:05:00.334 --> 00:05:01.901
-They're cool-looking, right?
-Like in sandwiches and on top

00:05:01.901 --> 00:05:03.033
of rice and things.
-Cartoons.

00:05:03.033 --> 00:05:04.801
-Cartoons, yeah.
-Yeah, yeah, they look nice,

00:05:04.801 --> 00:05:07.234
but scrambled is the way
we prepared them tonight.

00:05:07.234 --> 00:05:08.000
-[ Inhales sharply ]

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:09.767
Okay.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:09.767 --> 00:05:11.934
But they're not -- they're not
runny or anything, right?

00:05:11.934 --> 00:05:14.067
[ The Roots play
whimsical tune ]
-Hold on.

00:05:14.067 --> 00:05:15.501
No.

00:05:15.501 --> 00:05:17.400
They're not runny.
Do you want to see them?

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:18.901
-Yeah!
-Okay, good.

00:05:18.901 --> 00:05:20.634
[ Laughter ]
I want to open them over here.

00:05:20.634 --> 00:05:23.000
I just don't want you to get
a waft and get and then --

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:24.434
[ Laughter ]
and then bail.
-But see --

00:05:24.434 --> 00:05:26.801
how do you eat eggs,
if that's how you have to --

00:05:26.801 --> 00:05:29.133
I normally don't have them
under a silver container.

00:05:29.133 --> 00:05:32.934
It was like this is very fancy,
fancy, scrambled.

00:05:32.934 --> 00:05:35.801
This is right from the
Waffle House from Florida.

00:05:35.801 --> 00:05:38.033
[ Laughter ]

00:05:38.033 --> 00:05:39.033
There you go.

00:05:39.033 --> 00:05:41.734
[ Oohs and laughter ]

00:05:41.734 --> 00:05:43.067
Now, these are scrambled eggs.

00:05:43.067 --> 00:05:45.033
They actually look really good.
Now, do you want --

00:05:45.033 --> 00:05:47.434
Do you want ketchup?
Do you want hot sauce?

00:05:47.434 --> 00:05:50.567
-Hot sauce.
-Alright, throw it on there.

00:05:50.567 --> 00:05:53.968
Tessa Thompson trying eggs
for the first time ever.

00:05:53.968 --> 00:05:57.033
[ Cheering and applause ]

00:05:57.033 --> 00:05:59.000
Alright. Take your time.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:00.367
Oh, yeah, so what do you think
of the consistency?

00:06:00.367 --> 00:06:02.200
-Ooh, there's moisture!

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:04.200
[ Laughter ]
A little spongy.

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:05.934
A little spongy.
-What is the moisture from?

00:06:05.934 --> 00:06:07.634
It's from the egg.
-It's like egg water?

00:06:07.634 --> 00:06:09.767
[ Laughter ]
-The egg --

00:06:09.767 --> 00:06:10.801
-Ooh!

00:06:10.801 --> 00:06:12.701
Okay. Alright.
-Is that what you do

00:06:12.701 --> 00:06:13.801
when you get nervous?
-Yeah.

00:06:13.801 --> 00:06:15.033
-Whoo!
[ Laughter ]

00:06:15.033 --> 00:06:18.067
[ Drumroll ]
[ Cheering and applause ]

00:06:18.067 --> 00:06:20.000
-You got it, bud.
You got this!

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:21.467
[ Whistling ]
Come on! You got this, buddy.

00:06:23.701 --> 00:06:25.834
[ Cheering and applause ]

00:06:25.834 --> 00:06:27.968
[ Rimshot ]

00:06:27.968 --> 00:06:29.868
Drink water.
Drink water. Drink water.

00:06:31.501 --> 00:06:32.834
Here's water.

00:06:32.834 --> 00:06:35.667
No, no? Okay, she's okay?
Hold on.

00:06:35.667 --> 00:06:37.200
Do you have a decision
or do you have anything?

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:39.934
-[ Clears throat ]
-No? Nothing.

00:06:39.934 --> 00:06:42.834
-Okay.
-Wow. That's it, you did it?

00:06:42.834 --> 00:06:45.133
[ Cheering and applause ]
-[ Laughs ]

00:06:45.133 --> 00:06:46.868
-That's it.
We don't have to bring it up.

00:06:46.868 --> 00:06:48.767
-Ahh!
-We don't have to talk
about anything. Wow!

00:06:48.767 --> 00:06:51.467
-But now it's like documented
that I have eaten an egg.

00:06:51.467 --> 00:06:53.033
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah. And how did it feel?

00:06:53.033 --> 00:06:55.567
-Now I got to let it go.
Actually, they're not bad.

00:06:55.567 --> 00:06:57.334
-There you go!
That's what I'm talking about.

00:06:57.334 --> 00:06:58.434
[ Cheering and applause ]
Eggs are delicious.

00:06:58.434 --> 00:07:00.601
[ Ring ]
They're not bad.
-They're not bad.

00:07:00.601 --> 00:07:01.501
-Wow!

00:07:01.501 --> 00:07:02.734
We started a whole thing.

00:07:02.734 --> 00:07:04.067
Now, every time you come
on the show,

00:07:04.067 --> 00:07:05.734
you have to try a food
that you've never had before.

00:07:05.734 --> 00:07:06.801
[ Laughter ]

00:07:06.801 --> 00:07:08.734
Can we talk about the movie,
please?

00:07:08.734 --> 00:07:10.601
-Yeah.
-Congratulations
on this already.

00:07:10.601 --> 00:07:11.501
It's getting great reviews.

00:07:11.501 --> 00:07:13.634
-Thanks so much.
-"Creed III."

00:07:13.634 --> 00:07:16.067
Can we set up what's going on?
It's you, Michael B. Jordan.

00:07:16.067 --> 00:07:18.868
Michael B directed.
-Michael B directing, yeah.

00:07:18.868 --> 00:07:20.601
-I know. I thought earlier,
I thought you said

00:07:20.601 --> 00:07:22.934
Michael B'Jordan
and I was like

00:07:22.934 --> 00:07:25.934
[ Laughter ]
"Do you call him Michael
B'Jordan, like an apostrophe?"

00:07:25.934 --> 00:07:27.701
It's a period, yeah. It's not
-It's a period.

00:07:27.701 --> 00:07:30.701
[ Irish accents ]
-Michael B'Jordan. No, it's
-Oh, Michael B'Jordan.

00:07:30.701 --> 00:07:33.567
-Michael B. Jordan.
-Yes, Michael B. Jordan.

00:07:33.567 --> 00:07:34.467
-What's He like to direct?

00:07:34.467 --> 00:07:37.234
-Oh, he's great.
He's so great.

00:07:37.234 --> 00:07:38.834
-And, being from Ireland,
how long have you

00:07:38.834 --> 00:07:40.634
[ Laughter ]
worked with him?
You pretended you're American

00:07:40.634 --> 00:07:42.100
in the movie.
-I know, no, I just

00:07:42.100 --> 00:07:43.434
-I know you're in Philadelphia.

00:07:43.434 --> 00:07:45.100
-Cobbled over.
-I know.

00:07:45.100 --> 00:07:46.634
-[ Own accent ]
Is is it interesting

00:07:46.634 --> 00:07:48.701
getting direction from a person
who's also acting

00:07:48.701 --> 00:07:50.667
in the movie as well?
-[ Own accent ] Yeah.

00:07:50.667 --> 00:07:52.534
-How does he yell cut?

00:07:52.534 --> 00:07:55.200
-So, that's what I wondered
for about a month and a half.

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:57.634
And then I discovered --
And then I discovered

00:07:57.634 --> 00:08:01.300
that he would do this really
subtle like little cut sign.

00:08:01.300 --> 00:08:03.300
-Oh, wait.
Oh, so out of the frame?

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:04.701
-No, no, no,
just like he would just like

00:08:04.701 --> 00:08:06.334
hide his hand and be like doop!

00:08:06.334 --> 00:08:07.667
[ Laughter ]

00:08:07.667 --> 00:08:08.734
-Really?!
-Yeah.

00:08:08.734 --> 00:08:11.133
-Is it left
in the movie anywhere?

00:08:11.133 --> 00:08:12.701
-Yeah, that's a good question.

00:08:12.701 --> 00:08:14.734
I don't know.
-He's like, I'll be
right back, honey.

00:08:14.734 --> 00:08:15.734
[ Laughter ]
Take care."

00:08:15.734 --> 00:08:17.367
-Doop!
-And you go, "Whoa!

00:08:17.367 --> 00:08:19.501
That's what Adonis does.
That's one of his moves."

00:08:19.501 --> 00:08:21.334
-He did it in the middle
of a very emotional scene

00:08:21.334 --> 00:08:23.033
and I was like, "Wow,"
but otherwise,

00:08:23.033 --> 00:08:25.467
he really was seamless.
I didn't feel like

00:08:25.467 --> 00:08:27.133
I lost my acting partner.
-I thought he crushed it.

00:08:27.133 --> 00:08:29.200
I thought he did a great job.
-Yeah. He really did.

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:31.000
The only difference is,
you know,

00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:32.667
he has a say, of course,
as the director,

00:08:32.667 --> 00:08:35.534
in anything that happens
in the film

00:08:35.534 --> 00:08:37.767
and he's very specific
about clothing,

00:08:37.767 --> 00:08:39.667
which I didn't know.
-Oh!

00:08:39.667 --> 00:08:41.534
-Yes. So, we never disagreed
-Like wardrobe?

00:08:41.534 --> 00:08:43.200
-about anything,
except, sometimes

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:45.367
what shoes I would wear.

00:08:45.367 --> 00:08:46.467
[ Laughter ]
-Really?!

00:08:46.467 --> 00:08:49.100
-Yeah. He's really like
down to the --

00:08:49.100 --> 00:08:50.133
He doesn't --
-He's like, "Your character

00:08:50.133 --> 00:08:51.300
would not wear this."

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:53.801
-He just has ideas
about ladies' fashion.

00:08:53.801 --> 00:08:55.100
[ Laughter ]

00:08:55.100 --> 00:08:56.667
-And how was he?
Was he correct?

00:08:56.667 --> 00:08:58.734
-Yeah...
Okay, so,

00:08:58.734 --> 00:09:00.767
[ Laughter ]
there was a party.
There was a party scene

00:09:00.767 --> 00:09:03.100
where I really wanted
to wear something specific.

00:09:03.100 --> 00:09:07.701
I wanted to wear all black
because I'm like secretly goth

00:09:07.701 --> 00:09:11.200
[ Laughter ]
and I never got to be
in high school, so now I am.

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:13.667
And he was like, "No, no, no.
I think you -- No.

00:09:13.667 --> 00:09:15.634
You're not going to wear that."
And I was like, "Huh?"

00:09:15.634 --> 00:09:17.133
But he was right in the end.

00:09:17.133 --> 00:09:19.033
-Yeah, he was?
-Yeah, he was.

00:09:19.033 --> 00:09:20.434
I can admit when I'm wrong.

00:09:20.434 --> 00:09:22.601
-Your wardrobe didn't take me
out of the movie.

00:09:22.601 --> 00:09:24.033
-Yeah.
-You looked gorgeous.

00:09:24.033 --> 00:09:25.801
-Thanks very much.
-Stunning. It was perfect.

00:09:25.801 --> 00:09:27.534
Perfect for your character.
-[ Laughing ] Yeah.

00:09:27.534 --> 00:09:30.300
Can we set up what's going on
with "Creed III"?

00:09:30.300 --> 00:09:32.234
-Yes.
So, in "Creed III,"

00:09:32.234 --> 00:09:34.934
you find Adonis,
who's played by

00:09:34.934 --> 00:09:38.334
[ Irish accents ]
Michael B'Jordan...
-Ah! He's one of my favorites.

00:09:38.334 --> 00:09:39.734
[ Own accent ]
...and myself.

00:09:39.734 --> 00:09:44.133
I play a singer
who now has stopped performing,

00:09:44.133 --> 00:09:45.734
but owns her own record label

00:09:45.734 --> 00:09:47.033
and is producing artists.
-[ Own accent ] Producer.

00:09:47.033 --> 00:09:49.801
-And Mike is stepped away
from the boxing ring

00:09:49.801 --> 00:09:52.567
and he's, essentially, you know,
looking after boxers

00:09:52.567 --> 00:09:54.300
and has his own gym
and they're successful

00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:56.567
and they're rich and they're
famous and they're happy.

00:09:56.567 --> 00:10:00.067
-And then ding dong!
-And then in comes someone

00:10:00.067 --> 00:10:01.934
from Adonis' past,

00:10:01.934 --> 00:10:05.734
who stands to sort of,
potentially, destroy everything.

00:10:05.734 --> 00:10:08.200
And he has to -- because it's
a boxing movie --

00:10:08.200 --> 00:10:10.100
get back in the ring.
-That's right

00:10:10.100 --> 00:10:11.834
[ Cheering and applause ]
and everyone goes nuts

00:10:11.834 --> 00:10:14.400
when they're watching it.
If you want to --

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:16.567
It's so exciting.
I want to show a clip.

00:10:16.567 --> 00:10:18.801
Here's Tessa Thompson
in "Creed III."

00:10:18.801 --> 00:10:20.300
Take a look at this.

00:10:20.300 --> 00:10:23.133
-So, Miss Producer,
when's your next show?

00:10:23.133 --> 00:10:25.901
-I don't really perform
like that these days.

00:10:25.901 --> 00:10:27.934
Yeah, I took a break
because I was --

00:10:27.934 --> 00:10:30.667
I was having some issues
with my hearing onstage

00:10:30.667 --> 00:10:31.968
and now I'm trying to be careful

00:10:31.968 --> 00:10:34.667
and preserve what I got left,
so...

00:10:34.667 --> 00:10:37.167
I'm producing.

00:10:37.167 --> 00:10:40.334
Yeah.
Not anytime soon. [ Laughs ]

00:10:42.234 --> 00:10:44.801
-Still hard to give
that part up, though.

00:10:44.801 --> 00:10:46.601
Right?
-Yeah. It's not the only thing

00:10:46.601 --> 00:10:48.767
I love, but it takes work.

00:10:48.767 --> 00:10:50.033
-Yeah, that's what
I'm talking about.

00:10:50.033 --> 00:10:51.968
Tessa Thompson, everybody.

00:10:51.968 --> 00:10:55.601
"Creed III" is
in theaters and IMAX now.

00:10:55.601 --> 00:10:58.300
Andy Cohen joins us
after the break. Stick around.

